Cell["1.4  The vocab-sequence trade-off", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"7ddaab61-f7d0-4a03-a249-c25e93870b8e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Train BPE with progressively more merges and the sequence length ",
 "drops as the vocabulary grows.  The shape of the curve is the ",
 "empirical justification for sub-word tokenisation.  The data ",
 "below was computed once and cached in ",
 StyleBox["part-01-tokeniser-and-embeddings/data/vocab_seqlen_sweep.json",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "; we add the character and word-level extremes from the loaded ",
 "corpus and plot them on a log\[Dash]log scale."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5d034339-c8d7-4913-acfc-aca0a8260481"],

Cell["\<\
sweep = Import[
  \"part-01-tokeniser-and-embeddings/data/vocab_seqlen_sweep.json\",
  \"RawJSON\"];
bpePoints  = {#[\"vocab\"], #[\"seqLen\"]} & /@ sweep;
charPoint  = {65, StringLength[text]};
wordPoint  = {Length[Union[StringSplit[text]]], Length[StringSplit[text]]};
ListLogLogPlot[
  {Prepend[bpePoints, charPoint], {wordPoint}},
  Frame -> True, GridLines -> Automatic,
  FrameLabel -> {\"vocabulary size |V|\",
                  \"sequence length n (tokens)\"},
  PlotLabel -> \"Tiny Shakespeare: vocab\\[Dash]seqlen trade-off\",
  PlotStyle -> {
    Directive[ColorData[97][1], PointSize[0.018]],
    Directive[ColorData[97][2], PointSize[0.025]]},
  Joined -> {True, False},
  PlotLegends -> Placed[
    {\"char  +  BPE sweep (0\\[Dash]4000 merges)\", \"word-level\"},
    {Right, Bottom}],
  ImageSize -> 850]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"67f08086-754d-48eb-a68e-da2338e1d05d"],

Cell[BoxData[
 GraphicsBox[
  TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 455.04}, {720., 0}}, {0, 255},
    ColorFunction->RGBColor,
    ImageResolution->{100, 100}],
   BoxForm`ImageTag[
   "Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True, 
    MetaInformation -> <|
     "Exif" -> <|
       "ImageWidth" -> 1000, "ImageLength" -> 632, "XResolution" -> 100, 
        "YResolution" -> 100, "ResolutionUnit" -> "Inch", "Software" -> 
        "Created with the Wolfram Language : www.wolfram.com", "DateTime" -> 
        DateObject[{2026, 5, 21, 15, 44, 32.}, "Instant", "Gregorian", 1.], 
        "TimeZoneOffset" -> 1|>|>],
   Selectable->False],
  DefaultBaseStyle->"ImageGraphics",
  ImageSizeRaw->{720., 455.04},
  PlotRange->{{0, 720.}, {0, 455.04}}]], "Output",
 CellMargins->{{Inherited, Inherited}, {5, 10}},
 TextAlignment->Center,
 CellLabel->"",ExpressionUUID->"4d94cb34-eb38-4132-b955-73deb3c52d69"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Figure 1.3.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "  Character (top-left corner: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"|", "V", "|"}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"c7aef066-eb91-4e83-82d6-003f80f7b5c9"],
 " = 65, ",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"769edaab-78ad-4729-897b-9ea04f61eaa2"],
 " \[TildeEqual] 1.16 M), word (bottom-right corner: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"|", "V", "|"}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"1339bd96-75a3-4628-a1e9-9f7b29af9869"],
 " \[TildeEqual] 14.5 k, ",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"d6200bcf-84af-42c3-931f-1915f73ecd1b"],
 " \[TildeEqual] 252 k), ",
 "and BPE (the curve between, one point per merge budget).  ",
 "The interior of the curve is where every modern tokeniser lives."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"956ef873-d292-48dd-bcc2-6a3f0f0e2ac3"],

Cell["\<\
Numerical picture: at 50 merges the sequence is already 769k tokens (a third \
shorter than character) for the price of 50 extra vocabulary entries.  At \
4000 merges, 299k tokens, six times shorter than character.  The curve \
flattens because later merges chase increasingly rare bigrams.  Practical \
models pick an interior point: GPT-2 uses 50,257 merges, GPT-4 uses 100,256.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"101a5338-c28f-420e-add7-fd771232ab05"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The information-theoretic shape of this curve is not accidental.  ",
 "Tiny Shakespeare is approximately Zipfian (the ",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"76cc0a27-cb38-4429-940a-a9989a738743"],
 "-th most frequent word has about 1/",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"2c3343f7-6ddc-49b1-ab15-3d31fda47154"],
 " the frequency of the most frequent), so the marginal compression ",
 "from the ",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"5cdb2718-74e4-449c-9b42-fea385d7e4df"],
 "-th merge falls as 1/",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"d36e5674-1759-4e55-b693-13c606a4a2a3"],
 ".  Total compression grows as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"log", "(", "n", ")"}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"665e0ce6-6fb6-4504-879f-7612eb7412f3"],
 " \[LongDash] the gentle downward drift toward the word-level ",
 "corner."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ed6dee43-2930-4f05-8f8f-6be3ad1ab5d5"],

Cell["1.5  Character-level as the small-model alternative", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"9f3a1de2-2f94-44fb-a74f-a20e010a211d"],

Cell["\<\
BPE is the right choice for any model with a parameter budget in the tens or \
hundreds of millions.  For a small model (say 1\[Dash]5 M parameters) trained \
on a Shakespeare-sized corpus, the embedding tables \[LongDash] one row per \
vocabulary entry \[LongDash] consume most of the parameter budget, leaving \
very little for the transformer to actually compute with.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b82ebd1f-f6b8-4520-b02e-104afa33485d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A character-level vocabulary shrinks the embedding tables by a ",
 "factor of ~60.  The trade-off is much longer sequences, but at ",
 "this scale the per-parameter modelling capacity is what matters.  ",
 "This is the choice Karpathy made in ",
 StyleBox["char-rnn",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " (2015) at LSTM ~1M parameters on the same corpus, and the choice ",
 "we will make for our from-scratch trained model later in this ",
 "notebook."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"22831599-cc04-4ca2-ad83-1fc3ccfb3b7a"],

Cell["\<\
charVocab = LLMAtoZ`CharTokeniser`charVocabulary[text];
charTokId = LLMAtoZ`CharTokeniser`charTokenToIdAssociation[charVocab];
charIdTok = LLMAtoZ`CharTokeniser`charIdToTokenAssociation[charVocab];
{Length[charVocab], First[charVocab[[1]]] // Quiet}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"0033083f-3ec1-4f00-9c65-4b35d6049fea"],

Cell["{65, First[!]}", "Output",
 FontFamily->"Courier",
 FontSize->12,
 CellLabel->"Out= ",ExpressionUUID->"2973a2af-ad8f-420f-ab66-c1f309b2c858"],

Cell["2.  Embeddings: integers to vectors", "Chapter",ExpressionUUID->"b8bb8f6c-00c1-4876-97da-2fc578132449"],

Cell["\<\
A transformer does not see token IDs; it sees vectors.  Once we have a list \
of integer token IDs, we feed them through two embedding tables in parallel: \
a token embedding (one row per vocabulary entry, learnable) and a positional \
embedding (one row per position in the sequence, learnable).  The model input \
is the elementwise sum.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2670fb24-0a62-4b4a-bdfe-c09d5b2a9cab"],

Cell[BoxData[
 GraphicsBox[
  TagBox[RasterBox[CompressedData["1:"], {{0, 667.}, {1000., 0}}, {0, 255},
    ColorFunction->RGBColor,
    ImageResolution->{72, 72}],
   BoxForm`ImageTag[
   "Byte", ColorSpace -> "RGB", Interleaving -> True, 
    MetaInformation -> <|
     "Exif" -> <|
       "ImageWidth" -> 1000, "ImageLength" -> 667, "XResolution" -> 72, 
        "YResolution" -> 72, "ResolutionUnit" -> "Inch", "Software" -> 
        "Created with the Wolfram Language : www.wolfram.com", "DateTime" -> 
        DateObject[{2026, 5, 21, 15, 44, 33.}, "Instant", "Gregorian", 1.], 
        "TimeZoneOffset" -> 1|>|>],
   Selectable->False],
  DefaultBaseStyle->"ImageGraphics",
  ImageSizeRaw->{1000., 667.},
  PlotRange->{{0, 1000.}, {0, 667.}}]], "Output",
 CellMargins->{{Inherited, Inherited}, {5, 10}},
