 CellLabel->"",ExpressionUUID->"5a10b1d8-d50b-4cd8-8786-59e4add50f46"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Figure 2.1.  Learned positional embeddings: each \
position id (1, 2, ..., n) is looked up in a learned table; the resulting \
vector is added to the token embedding at that position.",
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic]], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"763bc784-6406-429d-8062-\
1037f31a41a2"],

Cell["2.1  Why a sum, not a concatenation?", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"03328842-6e57-4267-9ca0-6e7d6f272adf"],

Cell["\<\
Two reasons.  First, with the dimensions kept aligned the transformer needs \
no extra parameters to combine them; concatenation would double the input \
dimension and require the next layer to be wider.  Second, the sum is what \
falls out naturally if you ask \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]what is the right \
basis-free way to combine two pieces of information that live in the same \
vector space\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \[LongDash] either is just a vector, and \
their sum is a vector.  The model is free to learn token- and \
position-information directions that are nearly orthogonal, which is what \
trained transformers tend to do in practice.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4a23f365-a6b4-4735-9a15-efbd7f4d08d6"],

Cell["2.2  The embedder NetGraph", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"8a7c4d76-0ddb-49a4-a93e-792c0bc8f491"],

Cell["\<\
embedder = LLMAtoZ`Tokeniser`tokenPositionEmbedder[
  Length[vocab], 64, 256, RandomSeeding -> 42];
embedder\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"53f0e8d8-fcb3-4243-a630-65663ea7cec5"],

